 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on in the body",
   "text": "According to HPO, Canavan disease is caused by changes in a gene called ASPA [E96abfe0]. A paper reports that this gene normally makes an enzyme called aspartoacylase, which breaks down a brain substance called NAA (N-acetylaspartate) into smaller pieces [E66c3a16]. A paper reports that when this enzyme is missing or does not work, NAA builds up in the brain, and this is tied to a serious brain condition called leukodystrophy along with spongy changes in brain tissue [E4881c9c]. A paper reports that this is also linked to loss of the brain's white matter (the coating around nerves), called demyelination, which can affect how nerve signals travel [E6ac495a]. A paper reports that many of the gene changes that cause Canavan disease make the ASPA protein fold incorrectly, so it gets broken down inside cells, a process called ER stress [E1fc7967]. A paper reports that Canavan disease follows an autosomal-recessive pattern, meaning it is caused by the aspartoacylase enzyme being switched off due to gene mutations [Ead5ba8f].",
   "citations": [
    "E96abfe0",
    "E66c3a16",
    "E4881c9c",
    "E6ac495a",
    "E1fc7967",
    "Ead5ba8f"
   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Similar diseases",
   "text": "According to Reactome, the ASPA gene that is involved in Canavan disease is part of a pathway called 'aspartate and asparagine metabolism,' which helps process certain building blocks in the body [E493ee1f].",
   "citations": [
    "E493ee1f"
   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What research and support already exists",
   "text": "According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a trial called CANaspire is testing a gene therapy called AAV9 BBP-812 for Canavan disease and is currently recruiting at a site in the United States [E67c6e7d, E9d749ee, Eeb80c0a]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, another trial is testing a gene therapy called rAAV-Olig001-ASPA, along with the medicines Levetiracetam and Prednisone, and is enrolling children with Canavan disease by invitation at a site in the United States [Ea59ea0a, Eaee5b3a, E17dc012, Ebbfe1ee, Ee7ea317]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a completed study called CANinform gathered natural history information about Canavan disease at sites in the United States and Germany [E2631a0a, E0bdbf8c, E9f1f719]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, Aspa Therapeutics runs both the CANaspire trial and the completed CANinform study [Ecbfa1a8, Ee004059], and Myrtelle Inc. runs the rAAV-Olig001-ASPA trial [Eae7d4f0]. A paper reports that the National Tay-Sachs & Allied Diseases Association leads worldwide efforts around Tay-Sachs, Canavan, GM1, and Sandhoff diseases [E960696e], and that Canavan Disease Research directs funding toward developing therapies for Canavan disease [E6a703de]. A paper reports that the Canavan Foundation works to educate at-risk populations about Canavan disease [E7f03be6]. According to NIH RePORTER, the NIH NINDS funds a grant on oligodendrocyte-focused gene therapy strategies for Canavan disease and other leukodystrophies, led by researcher Guangping Gao [E8fe4b6c, E7370725, Edad7c03]. According to NIH RePORTER, the NIH NINDS also funds an observational study grant run alongside a gene therapy trial in Canavan disease, led by researcher Florian S Eichler [E5b19b6e, E0036970, E90bada5], and a grant developing NAT8L inhibitors for Canavan disease, led by researchers Barbara Stauch Slusher and Takashi Tsukamoto [E82dbb21, E6f21f7a, E09c722f].",
